<decompilertest>
<!--
    tailcallsaved: a run that raises the stack pointer without loading a single
    byte back through it is cdecl argument cleanup, not a frame teardown, so it
    is not evidence of a tail call.  `tailcallframe` (DIV-109, default on) reads
    a direct `jmp` as a tail call when the straight-line run ending at it raises
    the stack pointer by exactly what the run leaving the entry address lowered
    it by.  Both scans stop at the first control-flow op, so on a cdecl caller
    the backward one measures the `add esp,8` that discards the two arguments of
    the `call` above it and never sees the two pushes that put them there.
    Stage model: S2 flow classification (`xref_control_flow`, the CPUI_BRANCH
    arm), the `kuna_tailcallsaved` predicate inside `kuna_tailcallframe`.

    Layout (linked flat), 32-bit cdecl:
      caller @ 0x100040 : push ebx; push esi          (frame = -8, saved = 8)
                          test eax,eax; jz L2
                          push 1; push 2; call helper
                          add esp,8                   (teardown = +8, restored = 0)
                          jmp JOIN                    // read as a tail call today
                     L2 : mov eax,0x1234
                   JOIN : test eax,eax; jz R; or eax,7
                      R : pop esi; pop ebx; ret
      helper @ 0x100074 : return arg0 + 0x5a5a5a;

    JOIN at 0x100059 is an ordinary block of `caller`, reached by both sides of
    the branch at 0x100044; ebx and esi are still on the stack when the `jmp`
    executes.  The teardown restores neither.

    Pass 1 (default, tailcallsaved on): the deltas still cancel, but the run
    ending at the branch gives nothing back, so the jump stays intraprocedural
    flow and the whole function is emitted, including `| 7` from JOIN.
    Pass 2 (tailcallsaved off, the bug): the jump becomes a CALL + RETURN to
    0x100059, so `caller` ends at a call to its own join block and everything
    after it is emitted as a separate callee.

    Assert #1 pins the false call to EXACTLY ONE occurrence across both print C
    passes: absent in the default pass (the fix), present in the off pass (the
    bug).  Assert #2 pins the warning that attributes it, the mirror image.
    Assert #3 pins JOIN's own arithmetic to the default pass only, with the
    bug it lives in the callee instead.  Assert #4 pins the helper call to BOTH
    passes, so the only difference between them is the tail-call boundary.
-->
